{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head 7a9e94db9a95c0b00df932fa2d4d878764f0ff29. Three rounds of evidence stand behind this: I blocked 6451b1c1 on a red gate, blocked 79138ad2 on a documentation anchor that could never flare, and both are now closed and verified by execution rather than by reading. Artifacts at this head: 63099813 (internal/kernel, named), 57afc7b2 (internal/app), a1f8e8e3 (cmd/gs), 33eff05e (docs).\n\nDIFF SCOPE. One commit, 79138ad2..7a9e94db, touching two files and nothing else: four frontmatter lines in docs/reference/gs/checkpoint-clear.md and a new internal/app/checkpoint_test.go. No production code changed since the head I already reviewed in full, so the whole of that review carries forward unchanged, and I re-confirmed its two load-bearing greps at this head rather than assuming them.\n\nMY BLOCKER, THE DOCUMENTATION ANCHOR: CLOSED. The page now rests on bbe37f00 and 803d9687. Both resolve in the projection, both are live with no retirement, both are stale:false, and they stand at the paths whose behaviour the page describes, internal/kernel and internal/app. Both name commits that main actually carries, which I checked with git merge-base --is-ancestor rather than by reading a commit field: cbddb3d8 and 62440dc6 are ancestors of main. That is what makes the page flare. When this branch merges, the successors published at internal/kernel and internal/app retire exactly these two, and the page moves. The retired predecessors are gone from the tree: grepping the whole docs directory for 835c3b3106502f352f9baf48e76cdf1edd2e522e and 6011fdfd2e4b6ffceb13638035a2d9bbd3413e57 returns nothing. Measured across the whole documentation set, zero of 48 distinct artifact citations now point at a retired artifact, where two of 49 did before, and both of those were this page.\n\nMY RECOMMENDED GAP, THE PARSER TESTS: CLOSED, with one honest qualification worth writing down. I ran four separate mutations against internal/app/checkpoint.go in a throwaway worktree, restoring between each and confirming green after every restore.\nDeleting the canonical byte comparison alone, by disabling only that clause and leaving the schema and commit checks standing, fails the new test on two independent subtests, trailing_newline and unknown_field. That is requirement (b), met cleanly.\nRequirement (a) is met as a property rather than as a line. Deleting only the explicit len(data) \u003e maxCheckpointPointerBytes check leaves the test green, because the io.LimitReader cap still truncates the read and the truncated JSON fails to parse. Deleting only the LimitReader cap leaves the test green, because the explicit check then catches it. Deleting both fails the oversized_canonical_JSON subtest. The two mechanisms are deliberately redundant, so neither line is individually necessary and removing either alone is not a behaviour regression at all. What the test detects is the loss of the bound itself, which is the thing that matters and the right thing to assert. I record the exact result so nobody has to take the word \"covered\" on trust: single-line deletion of either mechanism is green, deletion of the bound is red.\nThe test itself is sound. Its oversized fixture is built from maxCheckpointPointerBytes rather than a magic number and asserts its own size before use, so it cannot silently stop being oversized if the constant moves.\n\nEARLIER CONTRACTS, RE-CONFIRMED AT THIS HEAD. Grepping internal/kernel non-test files for os.Open, OpenFile, Create, CreateTemp, MkdirAll, Mkdir, Rename, WriteFile, ReadFile, Remove, Stat and Lstat returns nothing, so the Git-only kernel boundary holds and architecture.md's claim that the kernel performs no local checkpoint filesystem I/O is still true. validateGenesis is still wired into both OpenObserved and AttachConfig ahead of any path construction.\n\nGATE. GREEN at the exact head, run serialized behind /tmp/gitseq-docset.lock with no other go test running. go vet ./... rc=0, go build ./... rc=0, go test ./... -count=1 rc=0, every package ok, docset alone at 110s, and the three packages that failed at 6451b1c1 all pass: cmd/gitseq-mcp 64.3s, internal/app 58.8s, internal/service 26.4s.\n\nARCHITECTURE CONCLUSION. Affected layers across the whole change from main: Ordinary Git storage, internal/gitstore, untouched, no new storage primitive. Kernel, internal/kernel, contract changed and the change is recorded in the same head; it gains an optional host-supplied opaque selector and gives it no authority, every candidate still passing the complete identity, sequence, payload, rotation and signature path, and it performs no filesystem I/O. Application host and boundary adapter, internal/app, contract changed and recorded; it owns the bounded pointer file, the off switch and invalidation. CLI and documentation surface, cmd/gs and docs, one added command with its usage string, index entry and reference page, and five updated pages. The architecture.md update is complete and accurate for every one of them, and the final commit changes no layer contract.\n\nSECURITY CONCLUSION. No unresolved defect. The trust boundary is correct in the shape that matters: neither selector is authority, and an attacker who owns the pointer file can choose only which fully verified object is tried first, never what is accepted. validateObjectID runs before the first Git command that touches a candidate, so an option-shaped value cannot reach git. Sorting on an unauthenticated depth orders attempts only; a forged deeper candidate fails authentication and the loop falls through. The ref is updated before the pointer in both the write and the repair path, so a local persistence failure can neither strand a new checkpoint object unreachable nor block the recovery anchor, proved by a test that runs git gc --prune=now against a failing pointer. Untrusted local input is bounded twice and compared byte for byte against its canonical form, which I verified by execution: a trailing newline, reordered keys, added whitespace, an unknown field, a wrong schema, an empty commit, trailing JSON, non-JSON, a 5 KiB body and a directory at the pointer path are all rejected, and Store replaces a symlink instead of writing through it, leaving the link target intact, at mode 0600. Genesis is validated before it reaches a path on both entry points. checkpoint-clear needs no actor identity, but it can only slow local reads and cannot touch the durable sequence. One nit I raised and am not blocking on: the off switch matches the literal string \"off\", so GITSEQ_CHECKPOINT=false silently leaves checkpoints enabled.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION CONCLUSION. Nothing left to cut without weakening the conditions of satisfaction. Every cut I asked for was taken: the sequence read and validateNamedSequence are hoisted out of the per-candidate loop, candidates are cheaply parsed then sorted deepest first and authenticated and extended only until the first success so nothing is materialised twice, unchanged pointers are not rewritten and the test asserts exactly one write, and the redundant DisallowUnknownFields, second decode and Chmod are gone with the canonical comparison carrying the check alone. The two size bounds are the one remaining redundancy and I am content to leave it: it is two lines, it is defence in depth on an untrusted-input path, and the test pins the property rather than either line. On the question I pressed twice, the head now states each selector's own job, the ref as garbage-collection root and the pointer as process-independent application state, and does not overclaim that the ref was incapable, which is right.\n\nLocal and origin tips both read 7a9e94db, the worktree is clean, and all four artifacts stand at the exact head and are not stale. This approval is for this exact head and nothing else; any change to it returns the work to a fresh artifact and a fresh verdict. I did not merge and did not edit the branch.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:630998138a58944c562e984fa2964419dc6e5553","head":"7a9e94db9a95c0b00df932fa2d4d878764f0ff29","verdict":"approved"}}